  "summary": "I reconciled all five St. Brigid discharges under RBC-TOC-02 and wrote a progress note for each patient. Four new items arrived while I worked and changed three decisions; I corrected them and wrote an addendum note for each of those three patients.\n\n**Walter Kim**\n- Furosemide raised from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily, as the signed addendum says.\n- Potassium chloride stopped, per the discharge summary.\n- Home omeprazole kept. The pantoprazole was only a hospital formulary substitute, so I didn't order it.\n- Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin and aspirin unchanged.\n- Lisinopril: at first I kept it on hold because potassium was 5.4 on 10/21. A repeat test on 10/23 came back at potassium 4.7 and creatinine 1.21, which meets the discharge condition, so I restarted it.\n- **For you:**\n  - The follow-up task I made earlier (task-t0003) is no longer needed, and I couldn't cancel it from my queue. Please close it.\n  - An unsigned draft addendum suggests furosemide 60 mg twice daily. I didn't act on it because it isn't signed; you may want to confirm the intended dose with St. Brigid.\n\n**Terence Stark**\n- Carvedilol: I first raised it to 12.5 mg twice daily per the summary (\"Coreg\"). A signed addendum then arrived saying the increase was reversed before discharge because his heart rate was 48–52 with lightheadedness on standing. He is back on 6.25 mg twice daily.\n- Aspirin and insulin 70/30 unchanged.\n- **Flagged to you by message:**\n  - **Atorvastatin 80 mg:** his fills covered only about 24% of the 180 days before admission. Per policy I made no change.\n  - **Glipizide 5 mg:** filled at an outside pharmacy, prescribed by Dr. Desai at Valley Endocrinology. The discharge summary doesn't mention it, so I didn't order it. Please check with him whether he should still take it alongside insulin 70/30.\n\n**Earnest Roberts**\n- Lisinopril: I restarted it after the 10/22 test (creatinine 1.08, potassium 4.6). **A clinic potassium of 5.5 on 10/23 then arrived**, so I put it back on hold. You have an urgent task, due 10/26, to review the high potassium and the next result.\n- Insulin glargine, insulin lispro and rosuvastatin unchanged.\n\n**Gerald Whitcomb**\n- Lisinopril 20 mg restarted: creatinine 1.12 on 10/21 meets the discharge condition, and potassium was 4.4.\n- Amlodipine and atorvastatin unchanged.\n- The potassium the hospital asked for 3 days after discharge hasn't come back yet.\n\n**Lorraine Pruitt**\n- Metformin ER is still on hold. The only creatinine on file (1.21) was drawn in the hospital, and the condition requires a post-discharge result. You have a task due 10/24 to review her post-discharge blood test and restart metformin if creatinine is under 1.5.\n- Amlodipine and atorvastatin unchanged.\n\nI closed the four new inbox items with the actions taken. Nothing else arrived for these five patients; the older items in your inbox are for other patients.",
